# In-house text lib — fixed API

## Context

`app/src/lib/text.ts` is a CUSTOM library, not lodash/underscore. Agents
routinely hallucinate lodash-style helpers on it. Its entire supported surface
is exactly three functions.

## Rule

- The complete API of `app/src/lib/text.ts` is:
  - `slugify(input: string): string`
  - `truncate(input: string, maxLength: number, suffix?: string): string` —
    `maxLength` INCLUDES the suffix
  - `normalizeSpaces(input: string): string`
- NEVER import or call anything else from it. Helpers such as `capitalize`,
  `camelCase`, `deburr`, `titleCase`, `pad`, `escapeHtml` DO NOT EXIST.
- If a helper is genuinely needed: add it to `app/src/lib/text.ts` as a named
  export with explicit types, a one-line JSDoc, and cases in
  `app/src/lib/text.test.ts` — in the same call, before using it.
- NEVER change the existing signatures or behavior of the three functions
  (`app/src/lib/text.test.ts` pins them); extend only by adding new exports.
- NEVER add `lodash`, `slugify`, `voca` or similar packages — implement in
  this file instead.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "lib/text.js" app/src` → every imported name is exported by
   `app/src/lib/text.ts` (`grep -n "^export function" app/src/lib/text.ts`).
2. `grep -rnE "capitalize|camelCase|deburr|titleCase" app/src` → zero matches.
3. `cd app && npm run typecheck` → no "has no exported member" errors.
4. `cd app && npm test` → `text.test.ts` still green, including any newly
   added helper.

